  "_commit": {
    "id": "62de507903c392100a1aeca3f1cf28c2ba7c7642",
    "author": "Patrick Aboyoun <aboyoun.patrick@gene.com>",
    "committer": "Patrick Aboyoun <aboyoun.patrick@gene.com>",
    "message": "fix: guard sparse rowDeviances' Poisson path against stored zeros (0.99.12)\n\nrowDeviances(x, family = \"poisson\")'s sparse path (.poissonDevianceBlock)\ncomputed v * log(v / sz[j]) directly on a dgCMatrix's stored values with\nno zero guard, unlike the dense path three lines above it\n(ratio[x == 0] <- 1, so log(1) = 0). A dgCMatrix is not required to be\ndrop0()'d; an explicitly stored zero hit this unguarded branch as\nlog(0) = -Inf, so 0 * -Inf = NaN, which propagated through rowSums into\nthat gene's deviance and was silently zeroed by the function's own\ndev[is.na(dev)] <- 0 cleanup, reporting a plausible-looking 0 instead of\nthe correct value.\n\nFound while checking BiocDuckDB against an independently discovered\ndeviance bug of the same shape in an unrelated codebase. The production\nDuckDBMatrix method was never affected: its generated SQL already guards\nboth families with if (value > 0) ... else 0, so it never evaluates\nlog(0). Only an in-memory dgCMatrix with an explicitly stored zero could\ntrigger this (never produced by as(x, \"dgCMatrix\") from a dense matrix,\nbut not excluded by the class itself).\n\nA swept check of every other sparse/dense statistical computation in the\nBiocDuckDB suite (scran.R, scuttle.R, the matrix-stats files) found no\nother instance of this pattern: rowDeviances.R is the only file in the\nsuite that does manual arithmetic on a dgCMatrix's stored-value slot\nrather than pushing the computation down as SQL.\n\nAdded a regression test reproducing the exact counterexample (a 2x2\ndgCMatrix with an explicitly stored zero) against its drop0()'d and dense\nequivalents.\n\nFull DuckDBArray rowDeviances test suite: 0 failures (36 passed).\n",
    "time": 1793040386
  },
